s and more for helping fulfill  the the goals of precision medicine and  the sequel to system you know in the  context of how epic bio the technology  has evolved this is a slide showing the  throughput evolution over the commercial  lifetime of the company so about six or  seven years we have built on the  foundation of the high quality that I  mentioned in the beginning of having  long reads having high accuracy minimal  bias we have been able to then build out  that foundation with regard to  throughput increasing the overall  throughput by well over ten thousand  fold by now with the introduction of the  sequel to system increasing the average  read length but over a hundred fold and  so forth so then in April of this year  we launched the sequel to system which  has a smart cell a chip that has an  eight fold higher feature size so that  translates directly to an about eight  fold increase in the overall yield of  sequencing and this is what we call the  smart cell ATM and what that translates  to is the same kind of performance  equivalent performance with regard to  read length accuracy lack of bias and so  forth but now with the higher throughput  translating to reduce reduce project  times and lower cost and so forth and so  how the hi-fi reads now look in the  context of the sequel to system I showed  this slide before this is a  representative example of the yield that  you get so you can see that for these  types of amplicons and medium range so  maybe up to 20 KB DNA inserts the read  length distribution has completely  shifted to the right where most of the  reads are now in very long reads half of  the reads in this particular example are  [Music]  you know well beyond a hundred thousand  bases and in fact for this particular  example the overall throughput was 320  gigabytes half of the data was in reads  that were greater than 167 kilobases  about four million reads so overall the  raw throughput of the system is up to  400 Giga bases the average read length  up to 100 kilobases and so then how many  passes do you need on the sequel to  system to get to a hi-fi read so this is  shown in this graph and we actually made  some improvements but in this regard  relative to the previous sequel system  so now it takes only about three passes  to get to cue 20 or 99% accurate reads  and it takes about 8 passes to get to  cue 30 or 99.9% accurate reads so that  in the end then you end up with  depending on the insert size and the  particular experimental design you get  between 1 and maybe 3 million of HiFi  reads highly accurate cue 20 or greater  CCS reads the redistribution of CCS  reads is shown on the right so in this  particular example the average insert  size was 12,000 bases so it translates  to about 12 giga bases of high five  reads about 1.9 million bases altogether  and the average single read single read  accuracy now is 99.8% but you can see  that the mode of the distribution is  actually much closer to cue 40 so that's  actually 99.99% and the same minimal GC  percent and sequence context bias holds  as you've known for the pec  biotechnology over the years so this  we've just launched a system and is  coming online and the first service  providers are now providing service at  hudson alpha at mount sinai and at the  university  Maryland there is a recent preprint and  that I want to point your attention to  if you want to learn more about high  fidelity long reads from the sequel to  system in collaboration of Evan Eichler  also Howard Hughes Medical Investigator  at the University of Washington  comparing both with regard to variant  calling as well as de novo assembly the  HiFi of the new hyper approach comparing  it to the more traditional continuous  long read or CLR approach looking at  specific regions of the genome such as  segmental duplications or Paris and Rome  Eric regions and I like to encourage you  to check out this new preprint if you're  interested in more detail so with that  I'll clo